{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And they shall eat the flesh.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory (Pesachim 83a) said: ״אֶת הַבָּשָׂר״ — and not sinews and bones, horns and hooves. According to them, why did it say ״את״? What does it come to include? And it appears that it comes to include what they said in the Mishnah (Pesachim 84a): whatever is eaten by a grown ox may be eaten by a tender kid, and Rava explains it: whatever is eaten by a grown ox in a cooked dish, etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends according to the dispute of Rabbi Yohanan and Reish Lakish (Pesachim 84a) regarding sinews that will eventually harden: according to Rabbi Yohanan they are counted [as part of the flesh], and according to Reish Lakish they are not counted. According to Rabbi Yohanan the verse is explained in this manner: ״אֶת הַבָּשָׂר״ — everything that resembles flesh, together with them He spoke, and they do resemble it; and according to Reish Lakish, everything that resembles flesh in the aspect of flesh from its own side, just as flesh does not eventually harden, etc." "<b>And matzot with bitter herbs.</b> According to the plain sense of the verse, according to what we have seen that HaShem said that it requires roasting by fire and that it must be whole, etc., this will indicate that the supernal intent is to show a great distinction and freedom, and that no others have dominion over them. Accordingly, the bitter herbs that HaShem commanded are because such is the way of those who eat roasted meat to eat something sharp with it, for thereby it will be pleasant to the palate of the eater and he will eat with all the desire of his soul. Also through this the greatness of the pleasantness will be recognized when he precedes bitter herbs to his mouth. Also the mention of matzot is a particular that prepares the palate so that the taste of the roasted meat will be savored." "Now, these three elements together give a combined flavor, and it alludes to three things: the first is the exile, for they embittered their lives; the second is the departure, immediately and in haste, so that their dough did not have time to leaven in Egypt; the third, that HaShem passed over their houses, which is the aspect of redemption, for HaShem passed through the land of Egypt and severed the bonds of the harsh fetters that had hold over them, which is the aspect of the firstborn, as I explained the matter in its place (Exodus 11:5). And all three matters together were necessary, and without any one of them there is no miracle in the other two — for if there had been no exile, they would not have attained what they attained through the refinement and the purging, and all the more so according to what we have written in other places (Genesis 46:3), that the great nation itself that HaShem brought out of Egypt consists of sparks of holiness that were there and were found there, and the merit of their extraction is the bitterness [maror] with which they embittered their lives, and it is the sod of what is said (Ecclesiastes 8:9): ״עֵת אֲשֶׁר שָׁלַט הָאָדָם בָּאָדָם לְרַע לוֹ״. And had there not been the haste with which HaShem brought them out, they would have returned and sunk back into the refining and purging, and it is the statement of the Tanna (in the Passover Haggadah): ״Had He not brought us out, etc., we and our children, etc., would still be slaves to Pharaoh, etc.,״ as it is written (Exodus 12:39): ״וְלֹא יָכְלוּ לְהִתְמַהְמֵהַּ״, and the essence of the redemption is the passing over [pesiḥah]. Thus you find that all three together are the essence, and therefore Hillel was precise to wrap them together (Pesachim 115a)."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:27:05.179611771Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}